  * {{ margin:0; padding:0; box-sizing:border-box; }}
  body {{
    font-family: -apple-system, BlinkMacSystemFont, 'Segoe UI', Roboto, 'Helvetica Neue', Arial, sans-serif;
    background: var(--bg); color: var(--text); line-height:1.6; font-size:15px;
  }}
  .report-container {{ max-width:1200px; margin:0 auto; padding:20px; }}

  /* Header */
  .report-header {{
    background: linear-gradient(135deg, var(--primary) 0%, var(--secondary) 100%);
    color:#fff; padding:48px 40px; border-radius:12px; margin-bottom:32px;
  }}
  .report-header h1 {{ font-size:2rem; font-weight:700; margin-bottom:8px; }}
  .report-header .subtitle {{ font-size:1.05rem; opacity:0.85; margin-bottom:20px; }}
  .report-header .meta {{ display:flex; gap:24px; font-size:0.85rem; opacity:0.75; flex-wrap:wrap; }}
  .report-header .meta span {{ display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; }}

  /* KPI Row */
  .kpi-row {{ display:grid; grid-template-columns:repeat(auto-fit, minmax(200px,1fr)); gap:16px; margin-bottom:32px; }}
  .kpi-card {{
    background:var(--card-bg); border-radius:10px; padding:20px 24px;
    box-shadow:var(--shadow); border-left:4px solid var(--secondary);
  }}
  .kpi-card .kpi-label {{ font-size:0.78rem; text-transform:uppercase; letter-spacing:0.5px; color:var(--text-light); margin-bottom:4px; }}
  .kpi-card .kpi-value {{ font-size:1.6rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); }}
  .kpi-card .kpi-sub {{ font-size:0.78rem; color:var(--text-light); margin-top:2px; }}
  .kpi-card.accent {{ border-left-color:var(--accent); }}
  .kpi-card.accent .kpi-value {{ color:var(--accent); }}
  .kpi-card.green {{ border-left-color:var(--accent2); }}
  .kpi-card.green .kpi-value {{ color:#2f855a; }}
  .kpi-card.red {{ border-left-color:var(--accent3); }}
  .kpi-card.red .kpi-value {{ color:var(--accent3); }}

  /* Sections */
  .section {{
    background:var(--card-bg); border-radius:10px; padding:32px 36px;
    box-shadow:var(--shadow); margin-bottom:28px;
  }}
  .section h2 {{
    font-size:1.35rem; font-weight:700; color:var(--primary);
    padding-bottom:12px; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); margin-bottom:20px;
  }}
  .section h3 {{ font-size:1.05rem; font-weight:600; color:var(--secondary); margin:20px 0 10px; }}
  .section p {{ margin-bottom:12px; color:var(--text); }}

  /* Tables */
  table {{ width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:16px 0; font-size:0.88rem; }}
  th {{ background:var(--primary); color:#fff; padding:10px 14px; text-align:left; font-weight:600; }}
  td {{ padding:9px 14px; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); }}
  tr:nth-child(even) {{ background:#f7fafc; }}
  tr:hover {{ background:#edf2f7; }}
  .num {{ text-align:right; font-variant-numeric:tabular-nums; }}

  /* Charts */
  .chart-container {{ position:relative; width:100%; margin:20px 0; }}
  .chart-container canvas {{ max-height:400px; }}
  .chart-row {{ display:grid; grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr; gap:24px; margin:20px 0; }}
  @media(max-width:800px) {{ .chart-row {{ grid-template-columns:1fr; }} }}

  /* Findings */
  .finding {{
    background:#ebf8ff; border-left:4px solid var(--secondary);
    padding:16px 20px; border-radius:0 8px 8px 0; margin:12px 0;
  }}
  .finding strong {{ color:var(--primary); }}
  .finding.warn {{ background:#fffbeb; border-left-color:var(--accent); }}
  .finding.success {{ background:#f0fff4; border-left-color:var(--accent2); }}

  /* Callout */
  .callout {{
    background:linear-gradient(135deg, #ebf4ff 0%, #e6fffa 100%);
    border-radius:8px; padding:20px 24px; margin:16px 0;
    border:1px solid #bee3f8;
  }}
  .callout .callout-title {{ font-weight:700; color:var(--primary); margin-bottom:6px; }}

  /* Footer */
  .report-footer {{
    text-align:center; padding:24px; color:var(--text-light);
    font-size:0.8rem; border-top:1px solid var(--border); margin-top:20px;
  }}

  .highlight {{ background:#fefcbf; padding:2px 6px; border-radius:3px; }}
  .badge {{ display:inline-block; padding:2px 10px; border-radius:12px; font-size:0.75rem; font-weight:600; }}
  .badge-high {{ background:#fed7d7; color:#c53030; }}
  .badge-low {{ background:#c6f6d5; color:#276749; }}
  .badge-med {{ background:#fefcbf; color:#975a16; }}

  @media print {{
    .report-container {{ max-width:100%; padding:0; }}
    .section {{ break-inside:avoid; box-shadow:none; border:1px solid #ddd; }}
  }}
</style>
</head>
<body>
<div class="report-container">

<!-- HEADER -->
<div class="report-header">
  <h1>Global Fuel Price Trends &amp; Policy Impact Analysis</h1>
  <div class="subtitle">A comprehensive analysis of retail fuel prices across 84 countries, examining the interplay between Brent Crude benchmarks, taxation, subsidies, and regional economic conditions (2020–2026)</div>
  <div class="meta">
    <span>84 Countries</span>
    <span>7 Regions</span>
    <span>27,468 Observations</span>
    <span>Jan 2020 – Apr 2026</span>
    <span>Generated: April 2026</span>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- KPI CARDS -->
<div class="kpi-row">
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg Retail Petrol Price</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">${{R['avg_petrol']:.3f}}</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">USD/Liter (all countries, all weeks)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card accent">
    <div class="kpi-label">Brent-to-Petrol Correlation</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{R['corr_brent_petrol']:.4f}</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Pearson r (weekly means)</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card green">
    <div class="kpi-label">Subsidy Dampening Effect</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{R['dampening_pct']:.1f}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Price reduction in high/very-high subsidy countries</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card red">
    <div class="kpi-label">Regional Price Spread (2026)</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">${{R['spread']['2026']:.2f}}</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Max – Min regional avg petrol</div>
  </div>
  <div class="kpi-card">
    <div class="kpi-label">Avg Tax-to-Price Ratio</div>
    <div class="kpi-value">{R['avg_tax']:.1f}%</div>
    <div class="kpi-sub">Across all countries</div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 1: Global Overview -->
<div class="section" id="global-overview">
  <h2>1. Global Fuel Price Overview</h2>

  <p>Across 27,468 weekly observations from 84 countries, the global average retail petrol price stood at <strong>${{R['avg_petrol']:.3f}}/liter</strong>, with diesel at <strong>${{R['avg_diesel']:.3f}}/liter</strong> and LPG at <strong>${{R['avg_lpg']:.3f}}/liter</strong>. Prices exhibited extreme dispersion: petrol ranged from a low of <strong>${{R['min_petrol']:.3f}}/liter</strong> (Venezuela) to <strong>${{R['max_petrol']:.3f}}/liter</strong> (Hong Kong), a <strong>{R['max_petrol']/R['min_petrol']:.0f}x</strong> spread driven primarily by subsidy regimes and taxation.</p>

  <p>Brent Crude oil — the global benchmark — averaged <strong>${{R['avg_brent']:.2f}}/barrel</strong> over the period, ranging from a COVID-era low of <strong>${{R['covid_low_brent']:.2f}}/barrel</strong> (April 2020) to a peak of <strong>${{R['crisis_high_brent']:.2f}}/barrel</strong> during 2022. This <strong>{R['crisis_high_brent']/R['covid_low_brent']:.1f}x</strong> swing in the underlying commodity forms the backdrop for all retail price analysis.</p>

  <h3>Annual Price Trajectory</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="yearlyChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Highest &amp; Lowest Retail Petrol Prices (Country Averages, 2020–2026)</h3>
  <div class="chart-row">
    <div>
      <table>
        <tr><th colspan="2">Top 10 Most Expensive</th></tr>
        <tr><th>Country</th><th class="num">Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th></tr>
        {"".join(f'<tr><td>{c}</td><td class="num">${v:.3f}</td></tr>' for c,v in top10.items())}
      </table>
    </div>
    <div>
      <table>
        <tr><th colspan="2">Top 10 Cheapest</th></tr>
        <tr><th>Country</th><th class="num">Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th></tr>
        {"".join(f'<tr><td>{c}</td><td class="num">${v:.3f}</td></tr>' for c,v in bot10.items())}
      </table>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 2: Temporal Trends -->
<div class="section" id="temporal-trends">
  <h2>2. Temporal Price Trends &amp; Volatility</h2>

  <p>The 2020–2026 period spans three distinct energy market phases. The <strong>COVID-19 demand shock</strong> drove Brent Crude to a low of $47.97/barrel by late April 2020, pulling global average petrol to $1.485/liter for the year. The <strong>post-pandemic recovery</strong> saw Brent surge 53.5% to average $106.59 in 2021, while the <strong>2022 energy crisis</strong> pushed Brent to a high of $130.00/barrel and retail petrol averaged $2.505/liter globally — the peak annual figure until 2026.</p>

  <p>The Brent-to-retail correlation is remarkably strong: <strong>r = {R['corr_brent_petrol']:.4f}</strong> for petrol, <strong>r = {R['corr_brent_diesel']:.4f}</strong> for diesel, and <strong>r = {R['corr_brent_lpg']:.4f}</strong> for LPG. These near-perfect correlations at the aggregate weekly level indicate that crude oil price is the dominant determinant of global average retail fuel prices, though country-level pass-through varies substantially by policy regime.</p>

  <h3>Weekly Brent Crude vs. Retail Fuel Prices (Global Averages)</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="timeSeriesChart" height="110"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Volatility Analysis</h3>
  <p>Volatility — measured as the standard deviation of weekly global-average petrol prices — peaked in 2020 at <strong>$0.289/liter</strong> (the COVID swing), declined during the 2021 recovery, then surged again to <strong>$0.233/liter</strong> during the 2022 crisis. From 2023 onward, volatility progressively declined to a low of <strong>$0.048/liter</strong> in 2025 before ticking up to $0.109 in early 2026.</p>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Year</th><th class="num">Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Petrol Volatility (σ)</th><th class="num">Avg Brent (USD/bbl)</th><th class="num">Brent Volatility (σ)</th></tr>
'''

# Add volatility table rows
vol = R['volatility']
yearly = R['yearly']
for yr in range(2020, 2027):
    yr_s = str(yr)
    p_avg = yearly['petrol_usd_liter'].get(yr_s, 0)
    p_vol = vol['petrol_usd_liter'].get(yr_s, 0)
    b_avg = yearly['brent_crude_usd'].get(yr_s, 0)
    b_vol = vol['brent_crude_usd'].get(yr_s, 0)
    html += f'    <tr><td>{yr}</td><td class="num">${p_avg:.3f}</td><td class="num">${p_vol:.3f}</td><td class="num">${b_avg:.2f}</td><td class="num">${b_vol:.2f}</td></tr>\n'

html += '''  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Key Observation</div>
    The near-perfect Brent-to-retail correlation (r &gt; 0.99 for all three fuel types) at the aggregate level masks significant heterogeneity at the country level. When disaggregated by subsidy regime, the correlation for "Very High" subsidy countries drops to <strong>r = 0.975</strong> — still high, but indicating measurably more price insulation.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 3: Regional Comparison -->
<div class="section" id="regional-comparison">
  <h2>3. Regional Price Disparities</h2>

  <p>Regional fuel prices exhibit persistent and widening disparities. <strong>Europe</strong> consistently leads with the highest average petrol price at <strong>$3.702/liter</strong>, followed closely by <strong>Oceania</strong> at <strong>$3.655/liter</strong>. At the other extreme, the <strong>Middle East</strong> averages just <strong>$1.226/liter</strong> — a 3x differential driven by production subsidies, low taxation, and proximity to extraction.</p>

  <p>The regional price spread (difference between highest and lowest regional averages) has widened from <strong>$1.61</strong> in 2020 to <strong>$2.87</strong> in 2026, indicating that divergent policy regimes are amplifying rather than narrowing global fuel price inequality over time.</p>

  <h3>Average Retail Petrol Price by Region (Full Period)</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="regionalBarChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Regional Price Evolution (Annual Averages)</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="regionalLineChart" height="110"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Regional Summary Statistics</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Region</th><th class="num">Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Avg LPG (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Avg Tax %</th></tr>
'''

for r in R['regional']:
    html += f'    <tr><td>{r["region"]}</td><td class="num">${r["petrol_usd_liter"]:.3f}</td><td class="num">${r["diesel_usd_liter"]:.3f}</td><td class="num">${r["lpg_usd_liter"]:.3f}</td><td class="num">{r["tax_percentage"]:.1f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Regional Spread Trend</div>
    The gap between the most and least expensive regions grew from $1.61 (2020) to $2.87 (2026) — a <strong>78% widening</strong>. This trend accelerated during the 2022 crisis when high-tax regions absorbed commodity spikes on top of existing fiscal overhead, while subsidized regions partially buffered the impact.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 4: Subsidy & Tax Analysis -->
<div class="section" id="subsidy-tax-analysis">
  <h2>4. Impact of Subsidies and Taxation on Retail Prices</h2>

  <p>This is the most policy-relevant dimension of the dataset. The data segments countries into four subsidy levels — <strong>Very High</strong> (10 countries), <strong>High</strong> (12 countries), <strong>Medium</strong> (18 countries), and <strong>Low</strong> (44 countries) — revealing a dramatic and consistent relationship between government intervention and consumer prices.</p>

  <h3>Average Retail Petrol Price by Subsidy Level</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th class="num"># Countries</th><th class="num">Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Avg LPG (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Avg Tax %</th></tr>
'''

sub_counts = {'Very High':10, 'High':12, 'Medium':18, 'Low':44}
for s in R['subsidy']:
    sl = s['subsidy_level']
    html += f'    <tr><td><span class="badge {"badge-high" if sl in ["Very High","High"] else "badge-low" if sl=="Low" else "badge-med"}">{sl}</span></td><td class="num">{sub_counts.get(sl,0)}</td><td class="num">${s["petrol_usd_liter"]:.3f}</td><td class="num">${s["diesel_usd_liter"]:.3f}</td><td class="num">${s["lpg_usd_liter"]:.3f}</td><td class="num">{s["tax_percentage"]:.1f}%</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>

  <div class="finding success">
    <strong>Subsidy Price Dampening Effect:</strong> Countries with High or Very High subsidy levels pay an average of <strong>${{R['high_sub_petrol']:.3f}}/liter</strong> for petrol, compared to <strong>${{R['low_sub_petrol']:.3f}}/liter</strong> in Low-subsidy countries — a <strong>{R['dampening_pct']:.1f}% reduction</strong>. Medium-subsidy countries fall between at $1.659/liter.
  </div>

  <h3>Petrol Prices by Subsidy Level Over Time</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="subsidyTimeChart" height="110"></canvas>
  </div>

  <h3>Price Pass-Through: Brent-to-Retail Correlation by Subsidy Level</h3>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Subsidy Level</th><th class="num">Brent-to-Petrol Correlation (r)</th><th>Interpretation</th></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-low">Low</span></td><td class="num">{R['sub_corr']['Low']:.4f}</td><td>Near-perfect pass-through — retail tracks crude almost 1:1</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-med">Medium</span></td><td class="num">{R['sub_corr']['Medium']:.4f}</td><td>Very high pass-through with minimal buffering</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-high">High</span></td><td class="num">{R['sub_corr']['High']:.4f}</td><td>High pass-through, slight dampening</td></tr>
    <tr><td><span class="badge badge-high">Very High</span></td><td class="num">{R['sub_corr']['Very High']:.4f}</td><td>Measurably lower pass-through — most insulated from crude swings</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="finding warn">
    <strong>Diminishing Returns on Subsidies:</strong> Even "Very High" subsidy countries show a Brent-to-retail correlation of 0.975 — commodity prices still transmit significantly. Subsidies compress the <em>level</em> of retail prices dramatically but only modestly reduce <em>volatility transmission</em>.
  </div>

  <h3>Subsidy Impact Segmented by Income Level</h3>
  <p>The dampening effect is consistent across income groups but the absolute price levels differ significantly:</p>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Income Level</th><th class="num">Very High Subsidy</th><th class="num">High Subsidy</th><th class="num">Medium Subsidy</th><th class="num">Low Subsidy</th></tr>
'''

# Build inc_sub table from data
inc_sub = R['inc_sub']
for il in ['High', 'Middle', 'Low']:
    vh = inc_sub.get('Very High', {}).get(il, '-')
    h = inc_sub.get('High', {}).get(il, '-')
    m = inc_sub.get('Medium', {}).get(il, '-')
    l = inc_sub.get('Low', {}).get(il, '-')
    vh_s = f'${vh:.3f}' if isinstance(vh, (int, float)) else 'N/A'
    h_s = f'${h:.3f}' if isinstance(h, (int, float)) else 'N/A'
    m_s = f'${m:.3f}' if isinstance(m, (int, float)) else 'N/A'
    l_s = f'${l:.3f}' if isinstance(l, (int, float)) else 'N/A'
    html += f'    <tr><td>{il}-Income</td><td class="num">{vh_s}</td><td class="num">{h_s}</td><td class="num">{m_s}</td><td class="num">{l_s}</td></tr>\n'

html += f'''  </table>

  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="subsidyIncomeChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Taxation vs. Subsidies</div>
    There is a clear inverse relationship: Low-subsidy countries average a <strong>37.5% tax rate</strong> on fuel, while Very High and High subsidy countries average approximately <strong>15.0%</strong>. High-income countries with low subsidies carry the heaviest tax burden at 34.7% — reflecting the use of fuel taxation as a fiscal instrument in wealthy nations (e.g., European carbon/excise taxes).
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 5: Income Level Segmentation -->
<div class="section" id="income-level-segmentation">
  <h2>5. Fuel Affordability by Income Level</h2>

  <p>Paradoxically, <strong>high-income countries pay the most</strong> for fuel — averaging <strong>$3.547/liter</strong> for petrol — while low-income countries average just <strong>$1.389/liter</strong>. This counterintuitive pattern reflects two forces: high-income nations impose heavy fuel taxes (avg 34.7%) and are predominantly low-subsidy, while many low-income nations maintain subsidies as a social protection mechanism.</p>

  <h3>Average Fuel Prices by Income Level</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="incomeBarChart"></canvas>
  </div>

  <table>
    <tr><th>Income Level</th><th class="num"># Countries</th><th class="num">Avg Petrol (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Avg Diesel (USD/L)</th><th class="num">Avg Tax %</th><th class="num">Price CV</th></tr>
    <tr><td>High</td><td class="num">32</td><td class="num">$3.547</td><td class="num">$3.350</td><td class="num">34.7%</td><td class="num">0.424</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Middle</td><td class="num">29</td><td class="num">$1.594</td><td class="num">$1.471</td><td class="num">21.1%</td><td class="num">0.588</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Low</td><td class="num">23</td><td class="num">$1.389</td><td class="num">$1.277</td><td class="num">22.9%</td><td class="num">0.644</td></tr>
  </table>

  <div class="finding warn">
    <strong>Price Stability Paradox:</strong> High-income countries exhibit the <em>lowest</em> coefficient of variation (0.424), meaning more stable prices despite higher absolute levels. Low-income countries have the <em>highest</em> CV (0.644) — their prices are both lower and more volatile, suggesting less effective price stabilization mechanisms.
  </div>

  <h3>Petrol Price Trends by Income Level</h3>
  <div class="chart-container">
    <canvas id="incomeLineChart" height="110"></canvas>
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <div class="callout-title">Affordability vs. Absolute Price</div>
    While low-income countries pay lower <em>absolute</em> prices, this does not imply greater affordability. Fuel as a share of household income is typically much higher in low-income nations. This dataset does not include purchasing-power-adjusted prices, which is a limitation for true affordability analysis.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- SECTION 6: Key Findings & Policy Implications -->
<div class="section" id="key-findings-recommendations">
  <h2>6. Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>

  <div class="finding success">
    <strong>Finding 1: Crude Oil Dominates Retail Prices.</strong> The Brent-to-retail petrol correlation of r = {R['corr_brent_petrol']:.4f} at the aggregate level confirms that international commodity markets are the primary determinant of what consumers pay. Domestic policy operates within the margin, not as a replacement for market forces.
  </div>

  <div class="finding success">
    <strong>Finding 2: Subsidies Compress Levels but Not Volatility.</strong> High and Very High subsidy countries reduce average retail petrol prices by {R['dampening_pct']:.1f}% compared to low-subsidy peers. However, even "Very High" subsidy regimes maintain a Brent correlation of 0.975 — crude price swings still propagate, just at lower absolute levels.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> Policymakers seeking <em>price stability</em> (not just low prices) need mechanisms beyond subsidies — such as price smoothing funds, strategic reserves, or hedging programs.
  </div>

  <div class="finding warn">
    <strong>Finding 3: Regional Price Inequality Is Widening.</strong> The gap between the most and least expensive regions grew 78% from $1.61 (2020) to $2.87 (2026). Europe and Oceania consumers increasingly diverge from Middle East and South American peers, driven by compounding effects of taxation policy.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> International coordination on fuel taxation floors or ceilings may be worth exploring to prevent extreme divergence.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 4: Low-Income Countries Face a Double Burden.</strong> Despite lower absolute prices, low-income nations exhibit the highest price volatility (CV = 0.644 vs. 0.424 for high-income). Their stabilization mechanisms are less effective, exposing vulnerable populations to sharper price swings.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> International development support for fuel price stabilization infrastructure (hedging capacity, strategic reserves) would disproportionately benefit the countries most exposed to volatility.
  </div>

  <div class="finding">
    <strong>Finding 5: Taxation Drives the High-Income Price Premium.</strong> High-income countries average a 34.7% tax rate on fuel — 60% higher than middle- and low-income nations (~21–23%). This fiscal overhead, not commodity costs, explains why wealthy nations pay 2.5x more per liter.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> Tax design matters. Revenue-recycling mechanisms (e.g., returning carbon tax revenue as dividends) can maintain fiscal incentives while managing consumer impact.
  </div>

  <div class="finding success">
    <strong>Finding 6: The Subsidy-Tax Nexus Is the Key Policy Lever.</strong> The data reveals a clear spectrum: Very High subsidy / ~15% tax countries (petrol ~$0.15/L) to Low subsidy / ~38% tax countries (petrol ~$3.45/L). The 23x price ratio between the extremes is almost entirely policy-driven, not commodity-driven.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> Subsidy reform and tax calibration are the most powerful tools governments have to influence consumer fuel costs. Gradual, transparent adjustments with targeted transfers to vulnerable groups represent the consensus best-practice approach.
  </div>

  <div class="finding warn">
    <strong>Finding 7: 2020 Provided a Natural Experiment.</strong> The COVID-19 demand shock (Brent falling 31% from January to April 2020) revealed that subsidy countries delayed or muted retail price drops — consistent with "sticky downward" administered pricing. Low-subsidy countries tracked the decline much more closely, delivering faster relief to consumers.
    <br><em>Policy implication:</em> Subsidy regimes should incorporate symmetric adjustment mechanisms that pass through crude price <em>decreases</em> as effectively as they buffer increases.
  </div>
</div>

<!-- DATA LIMITATIONS -->
<div class="section">
  <h2>Data Limitations</h2>
  <ul style="margin-left:20px; color:var(--text);">
    <li><strong>Tax percentage variability:</strong> The tax_percentage column shows high week-to-week variation within individual countries (e.g., US ranging 0–65%), likely reflecting estimation methodology. Country-level tax analysis should be interpreted as indicative rather than precise.</li>
    <li><strong>No purchasing-power adjustment:</strong> All prices are in nominal USD. Lower absolute prices in low-income countries do not necessarily imply greater affordability.</li>
    <li><strong>Binary vs. gradient subsidies:</strong> The dataset uses categorical subsidy levels rather than continuous subsidy amounts, limiting granular analysis of subsidy efficiency.</li>
    <li><strong>Data through April 2026 only:</strong> The 2026 data year is incomplete (Jan–Apr), and statistics for 2026 should be compared cautiously with full-year figures.</li>
  </ul>
</div>

<div class="report-footer">
